<div class="html" id="tag">
  

  
    <p class="reference alert alert-info"> <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">II Definition 6.2.3</span>, <a href="/bibliography/EGA">EGA</a>]</span> 
  

  

  <article class="env-definition" id="01TD">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01TD">Definition <span data-tag="01TD">29.21.1</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $f$ is <em>quasi-finite at a point $x \in X$</em> if there exist an affine neighbourhood $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) = U \subset X$ of $x$ and an affine open $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) = V \subset S$ such that $f(U) \subset V$, the ring map $R \to A$ is of finite type, and $R \to A$ is quasi-finite at the prime of $A$ corresponding to $x$ (see above). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $f$ is <em>locally quasi-finite</em> if $f$ is quasi-finite at every point $x$ of $X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $f$ is <em>quasi-finite</em> if $f$ is of finite type and every point $x$ is an isolated point of its fibre. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>

  <script type="text/javascript">
